<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">A speculative essay &middot; matter, antimatter, and the unfinished cancellation</p>
  <h1>The Residue That Did Not Meet Its Opposite</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Perhaps matter did not defeat antimatter. Perhaps both were residues of an earlier
    interaction, deposited into different modes of the same geometry, and what survived was simply what
    could no longer reach its complement.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The proposal in one sentence</p>
    <h2 id="t-residue">Matter as Residue</h2>
    <p class="lede">The visible universe may be made not from the substance that won, but from the tiny
      remainder that failed to cancel.</p>
    <p>Write the early populations as one large matched part and one small difference:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      n_B=n_0+\delta,
      \qquad
      n_{\bar B}=n_0.
    \]</p>
    <p>Pair annihilation removes the common term \(n_0\). What remains is the signed residue</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      n_B-n_{\bar B}=\delta,
      \qquad
      \eta_B=\frac{n_B-n_{\bar B}}{n_\gamma}\approx6\times10^{-10}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The broad physical picture is established: the observable cosmos contains roughly one surviving
      baryon per billion photons, and ordinary matter&ndash;antimatter annihilation removes matched pairs rather
      than changing their signed difference. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> What is not established is the
      interpretation offered here: that the missing complement may still exist in another phase sector and
      that the residue records an interaction earlier than the cancellation we can reconstruct.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading on this page: <span class="sci">&#9670; established physics or
      mathematics</span> &middot; <span class="spec">&#9670; a conditional mechanism</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; the framework's cosmological reading</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">A reversible warning</p>
    <h2 id="t-baker">What the Baker&rsquo;s Map Does &mdash; and Does Not &mdash; Give Us</h2>
    <p>Begin with twenty thousand points gathered into one small blob. Put them through an area-preserving,
      reversible baker&rsquo;s map and measure their Shannon entropy on a fixed grid. In the numerical run that
      prompted this essay, the coarse-grained entropy rose by <strong>8.323 bits forwards</strong> and
      <strong>8.321 bits backwards</strong>. The asymmetry was only <strong>0.002 bits</strong>.</p>
    <p>The map has no preferred temporal direction. It stretches and folds the blob into filaments whichever
      way its clock is run. Once those filaments become thinner than the grid, the observer counts a spread
      rather than a pattern. Fine-grained information remains; coarse-grained access to it does not.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      S_{\mathrm{fine}}(t)=S_{\mathrm{fine}}(0),
      \qquad
      S_{\mathrm{grid}}(\pm t)>S_{\mathrm{grid}}(0).
    \]</p>
    <p>The almost equal increases say that the arrow lies in the specially prepared blob and in the grid
      through which it is viewed, not in the reversible map. The small numerical difference is consistent
      with finite sampling and binning; it is not a dynamical arrow.</p>
    <p>This matters because it demonstrates a possibility often lost in the language of beginnings: a
      macroscopic asymmetry can be the visible history of a boundary condition under symmetric rules. But it
      also sets a limit. Entropy is an unsigned measure of inaccessible detail. Baryon number is signed. A
      reversible mixer can hide where the red and blue points went; if it preserves their colours, it cannot
      turn equal numbers of them into a net excess.</p>
    <p class="lede">The baker&rsquo;s map can explain how a residue becomes unreadable as an interaction. It
      cannot, by itself, explain why the residue carries the sign of matter.</p>
  </div>
</section>

