<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The earlier event</p>
    <h2 id="t-prior-interaction">A Cancellation Can Preserve a Difference; It Cannot Create One</h2>
    <p>Suppose an earlier interaction produced a complementary pair:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      X\longrightarrow M_A+\bar M_B,
      \qquad
      B_A+B_B=0.
    \]</p>
    <p>An observer confined to sector \(A\) measures \(B_A>0\). An observer confined to \(B\) measures the
      opposite. The complete state remains balanced even though neither local history looks balanced. On this
      reading, the question is no longer why matter defeated antimatter. It is why the interaction correlated
      the sign of baryon number with the sector into which each residue was deposited.</p>
    <p>There are several places such an event could be imagined: an early symmetry-breaking transition, a
      bounce from an earlier cosmological phase, a collision between branches, or the passage through the
      framework&rsquo;s Möbius seam. Those are not equivalent hypotheses, and none has evidence supplied by the
      geometry alone. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>Nor does annihilation solve the sorting problem. The reaction</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      B+\bar B\longrightarrow\text{radiation}
    \]</p>
    <p>removes one from each side and therefore preserves \(B-\bar B\). A non-zero global residue requires
      baryon-number-changing physics. A globally balanced but locally divided residue requires a separation
      mechanism. The framework may choose either debt, but it cannot decline both.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">What phase must mean</p>
    <h2 id="t-overlap">An Angle Is Not Yet a Wall</h2>
    <p>A global complex phase cannot protect antimatter. If</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      |\bar\psi\rangle=e^{i\phi}|\psi\rangle,
    \]</p>
    <p>then the magnitude of the overlap is unchanged:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \left|\langle\psi|\bar\psi\rangle\right|^2
      =\left|e^{i\phi}\right|^2=1.
    \]</p>
    <p>Ordinary matter and antimatter still annihilate when their states meet. For &ldquo;out of phase&rdquo; to do
      physical work, phase must label different internal modes, topological sheets, vacuum sectors or values
      of an additional coordinate. The annihilation rate can then be written conditionally as</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Gamma_{\mathrm{ann}}
      =n_Mn_{\bar M}\langle\sigma v\rangle\,
      \mathcal O(\Delta\theta),
      \qquad 0\leq\mathcal O\leq1,
    \]</p>
    <p>where \(\mathcal O\) is a real overlap or selection factor. The three possibilities are now precise:
      \(\mathcal O\) can vanish permanently, open periodically, or return arbitrarily close to an opening
      without ever exactly repeating.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Possibility one</p>
    <h2 id="t-orthogonal">Permanent Orthogonality</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">The two residues occupy modes that no ordinary annihilation operator can join.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>The mechanism</h3>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \langle\chi_M|\chi_{\bar M}\rangle=0,
        \qquad
        \mathcal O=0.
      \]</p>
      <p>The sector label is conserved. Matter and complementary antimatter can coexist in the complete
        geometry without sharing an electromagnetic or strong-interaction channel. Their separation is not
        distance but orthogonality.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>What it buys</h3>
      <p>This is the cleanest protection against annihilation. It permits exact global balance, explains the
        absence of an annihilation boundary in our sky, and does not depend on catching the phases at a
        fortunate moment.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>What might cross</h3>
      <p>If both sectors contribute stress-energy to one metric, then</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        G_{\mu\nu}=8\pi G
        \left(T^{A}_{\mu\nu}+T^{B}_{\mu\nu}\right).
      \]</p>
      <p>The complement could gravitate while remaining dark to ordinary light. It would resemble a hidden
        or mirror sector, not laboratory antimatter placed behind an invisible screen.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>The debt</h3>
      <p>The theory must derive the conserved label, the forbidden interaction matrix element and the shared
        gravitational coupling. Without those, &ldquo;another plane&rdquo; only renames the absence of annihilation.
        <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Possibility two</p>
    <h2 id="t-periodic">Periodic Overlap</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">The channel is usually closed, but the clocks repeatedly bring the residues back
      into contact.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <p class="kicker">Five against eight</p>
      <h3>The forty-tick window</h3>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \Delta\theta_{5\to8}(n)=\frac{3\pi n}{20},
        \qquad N_{5,8}=40.
      \]</p>
      <p>The return plane lags by \(27^\circ\) per shared tick, reaches opposition after twenty and returns
        to alignment after forty. If coupling follows alignment, annihilation or exchange would occur in a
        recurring forty-tick window.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">Five against four</p>
      <h3>The twenty-tick window</h3>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \Delta\theta_{5\to4}(n)=-\frac{\pi n}{10},
        \qquad N_{5,4}=20.
      \]</p>
      <p>The return plane leads by \(18^\circ\) per tick, reaches opposition after ten and alignment after
        twenty. The sign changes the handedness of the approach; the shorter period doubles the frequency of
        possible contact.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h3 id="t-periodic-cost">The cost of opening the channel</h3>
    <p>A gentle modulation is not enough. For example,</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathcal O(\Delta\theta)
      =\cos^2\!\left(\frac{\Delta\theta}{2}\right)
      \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
      \overline{\mathcal O}=\frac12.
    \]</p>
    <p>That would halve the long-term rate, not suppress it. In the dense early universe even a brief open
      channel could erase the residues. Survival instead depends on the integrated optical depth,</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      P_{\mathrm{survive}}
      =\exp\!\left[-\int\Gamma_{\mathrm{ann}}(t)\,dt\right],
    \]</p>
    <p>so the overlap windows must be extremely narrow, the cross-sector coupling extremely weak, or the
      first effective reopening delayed until cosmic expansion has reduced the density. The reward for that
      difficulty is falsifiability: periodic coupling invites searches for bursts, oscillations, conversion
      events or energy injection tied to one recurrence time. The framework does not yet identify the
      physical duration of a tick. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Possibility three</p>
    <h2 id="t-quasiperiodic">Quasiperiodic Separation</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">The scales never exactly rephase, but physical interactions need more than exact
      arithmetic non-coincidence to remain closed.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <p>If the phase follows multiplicative density scales rather than finite clocks, write</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \Phi_b(n)=2\pi\left\{n\log_b5\right\},
        \qquad b\in\{4,8\}.
      \]</p>
      <p>Both logarithms are irrational. Exact positive-integer alignment would require \(5^n=b^m\), which
        is impossible because powers of five and powers of two have no common prime factors. The phase never
        repeats exactly. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p>Yet the sequence is dense around the circle. For an interaction window with fractional-cycle width
        \(\varepsilon\), contact occurs whenever</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \left\|n\log_b5\right\|\lt\varepsilon.
      \]</p>
      <p>Near-alignments then occur infinitely often. Over a long run, roughly a fraction \(2\varepsilon\)
        of the ticks lies inside the window, giving a characteristic wait of order</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        N_{\mathrm{encounter}}\sim\frac{1}{2\varepsilon}.
      \]</p>
      <p>The events would be deterministic but non-repeating: not a forty-tick pulse, but irregular leakage.
        This may produce a very long-lived residue if the portal is narrow. It does not produce absolute
        protection. &ldquo;Never exactly equal&rdquo; is a mathematical statement; every physical interaction has a
        linewidth. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

